The event loop, verified:

```js
setTimeout(() => console.log('setTimeout (macrotask)'), 0);
Promise.resolve().then(() => console.log('Promise (microtask)'));
console.log('sync');
// Output — verified:
// sync            ← call stack first
// Promise (microtask)  ← microtasks before macrotasks
// setTimeout (macrotask) ← macrotask last
```

The model: JavaScript is single-threaded (one call stack — one thing at a time). Async operations (setTimeout, fetch, I/O) are handled by the HOST ENVIRONMENT (browser/Node), which queues their callbacks. The event loop is the dispatcher:

1. Run the call stack to completion (synchronous code first)
2. When the stack is empty: drain ALL microtasks (Promise callbacks, queueMicrotask)
3. Run ONE macrotask (setTimeout, I/O callback)
4. Drain microtasks again → repeat

The two queues:
- MICROTASK: Promise.then callbacks, queueMicrotask, MutationObserver — ALL run before the next macrotask
- MACROTASK: setTimeout, setInterval, I/O — one per tick

The consequence interviewers test: a long synchronous operation BLOCKS everything — no Promise callback, no timeout, nothing runs until the stack clears. 'JavaScript never preempts.'

The practical rules:
- Promise callbacks always fire before setTimeout callbacks (microtask > macrotask)
- setTimeout(fn, 0) is NOT immediate — it queues a macrotask; the current code + all microtasks run first
- The 'starvation' trap: infinite microtasks block macrotasks forever (the reason the spec separates them)

The binding rules, verified:

```js
// 1. Method call — this = the object before the dot
const obj = {
  name: 'Al',
  greet() { return this.name },  // this = obj
};
console.log(obj.greet()); // 'Al'

// 2. Plain call — this = undefined (strict) or global (sloppy)
function plain() { return this }
console.log(plain()); // undefined in modules / window in browser sloppy

// 3. Arrow function — NO own this — inherits from the creation scope
const obj2 = {
  name: 'Bo',
  greet: () => 'arrow: ' + this.name, // this = the enclosing scope (NOT obj2)
};
console.log(obj2.greet()); // 'arrow: undefined' — the arrow doesn't see obj2

// 4. Explicit binding — call/apply/bind
function introduce(greeting) { return greeting + ', ' + this.name }
const person = { name: 'Cy' };
console.log(introduce.call(person, 'Hi'));  // 'Hi, Cy' — this = person
console.log(introduce.bind(person)('Hey')); // 'Hey, Cy' — permanently bound
```

The four rules (in precedence order): NEW binding (constructor call) > explicit (bind/call/apply) > method call (obj.fn) > default (undefined/global). Arrow functions BYPASS all four — they capture this at creation and never change.

The arrow-vs-method distinction (the interview's core): a METHOD needs this of the object — use method shorthand. A CALLBACK inside a method needs the OUTER this — use an arrow. The classic bug: setTimeout in a method, using a regular function — this is lost (undefined), because the callback runs as a plain call. The arrow fixes it: setTimeout(() => this.save(), 1000) — the arrow inherits the method's this.

bind's permanence: bind returns a NEW function with this permanently bound — the bound function's this can't be re-bound (bind-ing again binds args, not this). The use: pre-bound methods as event handlers (const handler = this.handleClick.bind(this)).
